        "routing.number": "04 / Routing",
        "routing.title": "Declare routes where the controller lives.",
        "routing.lede": "RouteScanner reads class and method attributes, Router walks the resulting tree depth-first, and App::handle() attaches the matched parameters to a request copy. Immutable route-path segments are reused across dispatches; request parameters and custom matcher results are never cached.",
        "routing.exampleLabel": "Example/Controllers/GreeterComponent.php",
        "routing.inspectTitle": "A request through the tree",
        "routing.classPrefix": "class prefix",
        "routing.methodPath": "method path",
        "routing.capture": "capture",
        "routing.result": "RouteMatch -> request copy -> action",
        "routing.queryTitle": "Query",
        "routing.queryText": "Use getQueryParams() for query strings; route params and query params are separate.",
        "routing.matchTitle": "Path match",
        "routing.matchText": "prefix is the default; full rejects leftover segments at that route level.",
        "routing.methodsTitle": "Supported HTTP method attributes",
        "routing.methodsText": "All nine shortcut attributes extend the generic Route attribute.",
        "routing.behaviorLabel": "Matching behavior",
        "routing.behaviorHtml": `<ul class="checklist"><li>Class-level <code>#[Route(path: ...)]</code> values are prefixes by default; set <code>pathMatch: 'full'</code> when that route level must reject leftover segments.</li><li><code>:name</code> captures a decoded path segment and makes it available through <code>Request::getAttribute()</code>.</li><li>Query strings are parsed from the URI into <code>Request::getQueryParams()</code>.</li><li>Routes are checked in the order they were added to <code>Router</code>; the first matching branch wins.</li><li>A method can accept multiple HTTP verbs through the generic <code>#[Route]</code> attribute with a combined integer mask such as <code>HttpMethod::GET-&gt;value | HttpMethod::POST-&gt;value</code>, or by stacking distinct shortcut attributes such as <code>#[Get]</code> and <code>#[Post]</code>.</li></ul>`,
        "routing.failureLabel": "Failure cases",
        "routing.failureHtml": `<ul class="checklist"><li>No route match: <code>App::handle()</code> returns <code>404 Not Found</code>.</li><li>Unsupported request method string: <code>Router::processRoutes()</code> throws <code>InvalidArgumentException</code>.</li><li>Invalid custom matcher result: the matcher must return <code>UrlMatcherResult</code> or <code>null</code>, otherwise <code>RouterException</code> is thrown.</li><li>Non-public methods with route attributes are ignored and emit a warning during scanning.</li><li>A class-level route without any method routes contributes no route entries.</li></ul>`,
